The total momentum of the system is
$
  vb(P) = sum_(i=1)^N vb(p_i) = M vb(dot(R)),
$
so the centre of mass moves as if it is a single particle of mass $M$. Since internal forces cancel pairwise,
$
  M vb(dot.double(R)) = vb(F) = sum_i vb(F_i)^"ext",
$
so the centre of mass accelerates like a point particle subject to the external force $vb(F)$. In particular, $vb(F) = vb(0)$ implies $vb(dot(P)) = vb(0)$: the total momentum of a system is conserved in the absence of external forces. This is why an extended object such as the Earth can be treated as a point particle: its internal forces cancel out.
